@flexiarg operator/accept-with-one-amendment
@title Accept With One Amendment — 'OK, But … Should Be X Not Y'
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 3 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-GlUwss turn-KlWJtd turn-s7Rc0G
@parent snatch/accept-an-offer-that-beats-holding
@context The operator reviews freshly delivered work that is right in its frame but wrong in one attribute.

! conclusion: Lead with the acceptance ('OK'), then name the one amendment as a should-be-X-not-Y pair ('they should be foreground not background faces').

  + IF:
    The work as a whole is acceptable and exactly one design choice needs overriding.

  + HOWEVER:
    A bare amendment ('make it foreground') leaves the verdict ambiguous — is the rest accepted or is this the first of many changes? A bare acceptance lets the wrong attribute calcify. Bundling both in one utterance gives the agent the verdict AND the delta in a single turn.

  + THEN:
    Lead with the acceptance ('OK'), then name the one amendment as a should-be-X-not-Y pair ('they should be foreground not background faces').

  + BECAUSE:
    The parent's calculus — accept what beats the alternative of holding — specialised to the review verdict: the delivered whole beats restarting, so it is accepted; the amendment is attached because one attribute does not beat its alternative. The candidate adds the speech-act shape: verdict and delta in one utterance, so no turn is spent establishing whether the work stands.
